audits.design:
WHERE RLIMITS LIVE: on the kernel-graph `Task`, beside `nice`, `ioprio`, `oom_score_adj`, `keyrings` and `creds_ns` (crates/carrick-runtime/src/kernel/objects.rs:2331-2404). `Task` IS the Linux thread group (it owns `threads: Mutex<BTreeMap<LinuxTid, ...>>`), which is exactly Linux's own scope for rlimits: they live in `signal_struct`, shared by CLONE_THREAD, copied by fork. A `clone(CLONE_THREAD)` in carrick attaches a `Thread` to the existing `Task`, so thread-group sharing costs nothing and cannot be forgotten.

SIZING — and why NOT the shared arena. Putting `[AtomicU64; 32]` (16 resources x cur/max) into `carrick-kernel`'s `ProcessRecord` adds 256 B/record. The record today is 10xAtomicU32 + 4xAtomicU64 + AtomicU32 + AtomicU64 = 96 B under repr(C) 8-alignment, so 4096 records is ~384 KiB and the addition makes it 352 B/record = 1.375 MiB — +1 MiB of MAP_SHARED dirty pages per run and a 3.67x growth of that section. The megabyte is affordable; the design is not, for four reasons. (1) It buys nothing: the arena exists so a reader OUTSIDE the carrier — the NsSupervisor in its own host process, the detached FileAuthority helper — can see process state, and nothing outside the carrier ever answers a getrlimit. Every reader (prlimit64, getrlimit, the fd allocator, the FSIZE guard, the MEMLOCK guard, the SIGPENDING bound, the core-dump bound, /proc/<pid>/limits) runs inside the carrier with a KernelContext in hand. (2) It imports a hard 4096-process ceiling (PROCESS_RECORDS) and a second `ArenaError::Exhausted` domain into a subsystem with no capacity semantics. (3) `ArenaLayout` is `#[repr(C)]` and version-stamped (ARENA_VERSION = 4, attach fails closed on mismatch), so the change forces a version bump and re-qualification of every `carrick exec` re-attach for a table no cross-process reader consumes. (4) The fixed two-u64 encoding has no room for "never set" versus "set to this value", which the defaults table needs.

The alternative — and the recommendation — costs 256 B per LIVE thread group, heap-allocated on demand, with no fixed ceiling (the registry is `BTreeMap<TaskId, TaskRecord>`), no arena version bump, and inheritance handled by a call both fork paths already make. If an out-of-carrier renderer ever needs the values, publish a read-only projection the way `Registry::oom_score_adj_by_pid()` (kernel/core.rs:1115) already projects into the /proc context — never make the arena the authority.

TYPED DOMAIN (carrick-abi/src/lib.rs). Replace the hand-numbered `LINUX_RLIMIT_*` consts and `LINUX_RLIM_NLIMITS` with an ordinal enum, per the AGENTS.md rule that a new number table derives from an enum:
  #[repr(u8)] pub enum LinuxResource { Cpu=0, Fsize, Data, Stack, Core, Rss, Nproc, Nofile, Memlock, As, Locks, Sigpending, Msgqueue, Nice, Rtprio, Rttime }
  impl LinuxResource { pub const COUNT: usize = 16;
    /// The ONLY constructor from a guest register. `>= COUNT` is EINVAL here,
    /// so no caller can index the table with an unvalidated u64.
    pub fn from_guest_arg(raw: u64) -> Result<Self, LinuxErrno>;
    pub const fn index(self) -> usize; }
This also names the six resources the ABI currently has no constant for (Rss, Locks, Msgqueue, Nice, Rtprio, Rttime) and makes the `resource as usize` indexing at time.rs:963 unrepresentable.

STATE + API (kernel/objects.rs), copying the `process_credentials: ArcSwap<Credentials>` shape already on `Task`:
  /// This process's sixteen resource limits — the kernel-graph counterpart of
  /// Linux's `signal_struct.rlim`. Per-TASK because rlimits are thread-group
  /// state, and NOT a host-process value because under HVPatch every Linux
  /// process is a thread of one Darwin process.
  rlimits: ArcSwap<RlimitSet>,
  /// Serialises read-modify-write only. Readers never take it.
  rlimit_write: Mutex<()>,

  pub struct RlimitSet([LinuxRlimit; LinuxResource::COUNT]);
  impl RlimitSet {
    /// THE single default table. getrlimit, prlimit64, /proc/<pid>/limits,
    /// /proc/<pid>/status SigQ, the fd allocator, the FSIZE/MEMLOCK guards and
    /// the core-dump bound all read this one function, so the four-way
    /// contradiction between `rlimit_for_resource` (time.rs:1183), the frozen
    /// /proc/limits literal (vfs/proc.rs:4052) and /proc/sys/kernel/threads-max
    /// (vfs/proc.rs:509) becomes inexpressible.
    pub fn carrick_defaults() -> Self;
    pub fn get(&self, r: LinuxResource) -> LinuxRlimit;
    /// Render the seventeen-line proc(5) table, units and all.
    pub fn proc_limits_text(&self) -> String;
  }
  impl Task {
    /// Lock-free read of one resource. `ArcSwap::load` — a hazard-pointer
    /// guard, no allocation — so the fd-allocation and write hot paths do not
    /// gain a mutex.
    pub fn rlimit(&self, r: LinuxResource) -> LinuxRlimit;
    pub fn rlimits(&self) -> Arc<RlimitSet>;
    /// Read-modify-write ONE resource under `rlimit_write`, returning the
    /// PREVIOUS value. `decide` sees the current pair and returns either the
    /// replacement or an errno, so prlimit64's "report old, then apply new",
    /// its cur>max EINVAL, its nr_open EPERM and its hard-raise EPERM all
    /// happen inside one critical section and cannot race a sibling thread's
    /// setrlimit. Nothing else may be called while it is held.
    pub fn replace_rlimit(&self, r: LinuxResource,
        decide: impl FnOnce(LinuxRlimit) -> Result<LinuxRlimit, LinuxErrno>)
        -> Result<LinuxRlimit, LinuxErrno>;
    /// fork(2): "the child inherits copies of the parent's resource limits."
    /// Called ONLY from `inherit_fork_attributes_from` (objects.rs:2522).
    fn inherit_rlimits_from(&self, parent: &Task);
  }

REACHING ANOTHER PROCESS (kernel/operations.rs, beside `live_task_process_euid` at :1016):
  /// Resolve a guest-supplied prlimit64 pid to the TASK whose limit table it
  /// names, plus the credentials that own it. This is the authority Darwin
  /// cannot supply: under HVPatch every Linux process is a thread of one
  /// Darwin process, so `std::process::id()` is the same number for all of
  /// them and `kill(pid,0)` asks about a pid space Darwin never heard of.
  /// A TID is accepted and resolved to its thread group, as Linux does —
  /// rlimits are signal_struct state — reusing `live_keys_for_thread`
  /// (already used by `hvpatch_process_signal_target`, signal.rs:2567).
  pub(crate) fn rlimit_target(&self, pid: NsPid) -> RlimitTarget;
  pub(crate) enum RlimitTarget {
      Live { task: TaskRef, key: TaskKey, credentials: Arc<Credentials> },
      Zombie,   // ESRCH for prlimit64; a zombie has no signal_struct to edit
      Missing,  // ESRCH
  }
Dispatcher wrapper (dispatch/mod.rs, beside `guest_process_target` at :3569):
  /// 0 and the caller's own pid resolve to `cx.kernel.task()` with no registry
  /// lookup; anything else goes through `Kernel::rlimit_target`. Never
  /// `std::process::id()`.
  pub(crate) fn rlimit_target_task<M>(&self, cx: &SyscallCtx<M>, pid: NsPid)
      -> Result<TaskRef, LinuxErrno>;

PERMISSION MODEL — must land in the same commit. prlimit64(2): to read or write ANOTHER process's limits the caller needs CAP_SYS_RESOURCE in the target's user namespace, or its real, effective AND saved uid must each equal the target's real, effective and saved uid, and likewise for gids. Carrick today performs NO target-ownership check (time.rs:861-975 has only the hard-raise gate at :934-941, which reads the CALLER's task). That is harmless while the write lands on self; the moment target resolution is correct it becomes real cross-process clobbering inside the guest. Both halves of the comparison come from the same authority — the caller's from `cx.resources().credentials()` / `cred_snapshot()`, the target's from `RlimitTarget::Live { credentials }` — which is the same correction `guest_process_target` already made for `setpriority` (see its doc at dispatch/mod.rs:3557-3565).

READERS WITH NO `cx`. `nofile_limit(&self)` (fs/fd_helpers.rs:56), `fsize_soft_limit(&self)` (fs.rs:6809) and `rlimit_core_enabled(&self)` (proc.rs:4841) take no SyscallCtx. Do not thread one through; use the mechanism `cred_snapshot` already uses — `dispatch::resources::with_active_context(|cx| cx.task().rlimit(r))` (resources.rs:93), whose doc comment states this exact purpose. Outside a dispatch scope follow `cred_snapshot`'s discipline verbatim (creds.rs:263-274): fall back only under `#[cfg(test)]`, and `tracing::error!` + abort under `#[cfg(not(test))]`. A silent `unwrap_or(defaults)` there would restore the 1 Mi ceiling invisibly and let the new probe pass anyway.

INHERITANCE ACROSS FORK. One line inside `Task::inherit_fork_attributes_from` (objects.rs:2522), which BOTH fork paths already call and which exists precisely because they drifted before: `ForkReservation::prepare` (operations.rs:536) for the in-process HVPatch fork, and `reset_one_task_kernel_binding_for_current_process` (dispatch/mod.rs:3413-3415) for host-fork backends. Copy by VALUE — the whole point of TestPrlimitFileLimit is that the parent stays at 43 while the child moves to 42 — and delete `ProcState::rlimit_overrides` (dispatch/proc.rs:674, cloned at :846) so `fork_clone` can no longer carry a second, private copy. CLONE_THREAD needs no code: no new Task is minted, so `ThreadResources::for_clone` must NOT copy the table.

RESET ON EXEC. Nothing resets. execve(2) preserves every resource limit, and carrick's exec does not build a new `Task` (objects.rs:2600-2607 makes exactly this argument for creds_ns), so preservation is free provided no exec path "helpfully" clears the table. Three exec-adjacent items DO need decisions, and each is a real divergence today: (a) RLIMIT_STACK is consulted by Linux only at exec, to size the initial stack — carrick maps the static `LINUX_RLIMIT_STACK_SOFT` (carrick-mem/src/memory.rs:534 = 8 MiB) at vcpu_loop/mod.rs:1602 and reports an 8 MiB `[stack]` VMA at vfs/proc.rs:2757 regardless of what the task set, so a guest that lowers RLIMIT_STACK to 1 MiB and execs still gets 8 MiB of backing and an 8 MiB /proc/self/maps row; (b) the RLIMIT_CPU enforcement helper must be re-anchored to the exec'd task rather than cancelled, because Linux's CPU accounting is not reset by exec; (c) Go's `syscall.forkAndExecInChild` re-applies its cached original NOFILE with a raw `prlimit64(0, ...)` in the child between fork and exec, so the child's KernelContext must already be published when that syscall is serviced or the self-call lands on the parent's task.

/proc. Delete `synthetic_proc_self_limits()` (vfs/proc.rs:4052) and render from `RlimitSet::proc_limits_text()`. Add "limits" to `PROC_PID_FILES` (vfs/proc.rs:1999) and give `SyntheticProcContext` a `rlimits_by_pid: BTreeMap<u32, Arc<RlimitSet>>` filled from the registry — the exact shape of the existing `oom_score_adj: BTreeMap<u32, i32>` field (vfs/proc.rs:230) and its `Registry::oom_score_adj_by_pid()` source, so a pid absent from the map is ENOENT rather than a fabricated table. Delete `host_rlimit_nproc()` (vfs/proc.rs:506) and render /proc/sys/kernel/threads-max from a carrick constant matching the NPROC default, so the guest's three answers become one. Fold `NR_OPEN_CEILING` (time.rs:922) and the `/proc/sys/fs/nr_open` literal (vfs/proc.rs:763) into one `carrick-abi` constant.

OUT OF SCOPE, STATED PLAINLY. The RLIMIT_CPU carrier-scope defects (time.rs:1025 generation static, :1130 carrier-wide CPU sum, :1134/:1140 carrier-global PROC_PENDING) are a separate change — they need per-task CPU accounting and task-directed signal publication, both of which exist (`Task::TaskCpu`, `guest_cpu::this_thread_slot`, `Kernel::authorize_signal_target`) but are not wired here. Moving the table without moving the helper makes that bug LOUDER, not quieter, so the commit message must say so rather than imply RLIMIT_CPU is fixed.

MECHANICAL GATE. Add to .semgrep/typed-domains.yml, in the shape docs/host-facility-boundary.md's "Make it mechanical" section proposes: `libc::getrlimit` / `libc::setrlimit` are errors anywhere except the three named host-budget helpers (`raise_host_nofile_backing`, carrick-cli startup, `inherited_fd_scan_limit`), and `std::process::id()` / `libc::kill` are errors inside dispatch/time.rs. That converts "answer rlimits from the kernel graph" from a habit into a build failure.

audits.subsystem:
Process identity and relationships (pid, ppid, pgid, sid, existence) under the HVPatch unified kernel — `crates/carrick-runtime/src/dispatch/proc.rs`, `crates/carrick-runtime/src/namespace/pid.rs`, and the guest-facing consumers of both (`dispatch/abi_args.rs`, `dispatch/creds.rs`, `dispatch/fs.rs`, `dispatch/time.rs`, `vfs/proc.rs`).

THE STRUCTURAL FINDING, which reframes every individual site below:

`crates/carrick-spec/src/lib.rs:237` — `ExecBackendRequest` has exactly ONE variant, `HvPatch`; `native`/`vmm` are rejected with "were retired". `crates/carrick-runtime/src/hvpatch/mod.rs:1022` `root_bootstrap_identity()` seeds the root task at `LINUX_BOOTSTRAP_PID` = 1 (`carrick-abi/src/lib.rs:4149`), and `kernel/ids.rs` allocates task ids upward from there. So guest pids ARE small integers today — 1, 2, 3 — and the hazard `dispatch/mod.rs:3533-3542` describes as future tense ("It becomes actively dangerous the moment guest pids are small — a guest pid of 1 would probe the host's pid 1, which on macOS is `launchd`") is LIVE at HEAD, not pending.

Second, and this is the load-bearing one: `crates/carrick-runtime/src/threaded_loop.rs:226-228` calls `namespace::pid::init(std::process::id())` on every `carrick run` (PidMode::Private → `execute.rs:237/239` `request()`/`request_supervisor()`). That registers exactly ONE member — the carrier's host pid → ns-pid 1 — and `namespace::pid::enabled()` becomes TRUE. But the HVPatch fork path (`vcpu_loop/quiesce.rs:1786-1960`) never calls `register_child`; it publishes into the kernel graph instead. The consequence is that the whole `namespace/pid.rs` translation layer degenerates to a CONSTANT on the shipping lane:

  - `self_ns_pid()` (`namespace/pid.rs:352`) returns **1 for every guest process**, because it starts from `std::process::id()` and the carrier is the only registered member;
  - `ns_to_host_or_self(n)`/`ns_to_host_pgid(n)` return `None` for every n != 1;
  - `host_to_ns_or_self(h)` returns 0 for every h that is not the carrier's host pid, so `HostPid::to_guest()` yields 0;
  - `enabled()` is true, so every `if crate::namespace::pid::enabled()` branch in dispatch takes the arm written for the retired model.

That is not "an approximate answer" — it is a constant function standing where a per-process one belongs, and it is exactly the shape `docs/identity-and-scope-domains.md` calls "code that keeps compiling and keeps explaining itself in terms of a model that no longer exists". `dispatch/proc.rs:6-9` still opens with "**a guest process is a real macOS process** … The host process tree mirrors the guest process tree" and `:26` "Because guest pids ARE host pids"; both are false and both are actively steering maintenance.

The good news, which bounds the work: the primary handlers for `setpgid`/`getpgid`/`getsid`/`setsid` (`proc.rs:2860/2899/2934/2965`), `wait4`/`waitid` (`proc.rs:3445/2988`), and `kill` (`signal.rs:1420`) ALREADY branch to the kernel graph first via `this.hvpatch_process()`, and the kernel graph's `set_process_group`/`create_session` (`kernel/operations.rs:2399/2625`) are a faithful, reservation-and-revision-checked Linux implementation with the real EPERM/EACCES/ESRCH edges. The bugs are (a) everything that reads pgid/sid/pid OUTSIDE those four handlers, and (b) the host tails of those same handlers, which are dead code on the only shipping backend and must be deleted rather than left as a second answer.

batches.verification:
Host-only — no guest, no Docker, no HVF. This is the one batch that can land while the current conformance gate occupies the machine.

RED-FIRST unit test in `crates/carrick-runtime/tests/integration/syscall_creds.rs` (which already holds `prlimit64_and_getrlimit_round_trip_per_resource` at :1253 and IS executed by `just test-integration`; note carrick-cli has no lib target so an in-file `mod tests` there is compiled by clippy and NEVER RUN). Build TWO Tasks in one kernel graph and assert, each failing to COMPILE or failing at HEAD before the change:
- `rlimit_target(child_pid)` returns the CHILD's TaskRef, and `replace_rlimit` on it leaves the parent's `Task::rlimit` untouched, and vice versa;
- `inherit_fork_attributes_from` copies the whole RlimitSet BY VALUE (the parent must stay at 43 while the child moves to 42) and copies the nsproxy handles by Arc-clone for UTS/NET;
- `LinuxResource::from_guest_arg(16)` is EINVAL;
- a tid that is not a thread-group leader resolves to its leader's table;
- `process_identity` answers for a task that has been moved to the zombie table (ESRCH today) and reports `state: Zombie`;
- `process_group_exists(g)` is TRUE for a group whose only remaining member is a zombie — the regression guard for the `remove_group_member` subtlety, and the assertion most likely to catch a wrong fix.
Copy the shape of `set_process_group_enforces_parent_session_and_group_policy_atomically` (operations.rs:6879).

GATES: `just test`, `just test-integration`, `just lint-domains` (new baseline must be green), then full `just ci`. NO perf claim may be made from any run taken while the gate is live.

REVIEWER CHECK: `rg 'rlimit_write' crates/carrick-runtime` must return exactly two hits (the field and its single acquisition), and `replace_rlimit`'s body must call into no other subsystem — that is the structural mitigation for the lock inversion described in conflicts_avoided.
